Implementation file: src/policy.ts
Passing tests: 15
Recent Green phase: added `enchantmentSurchargeForItem` (30% surcharge when item.enchantment >= 5), mirroring the existing `curseSurchargeForItem` pattern exactly. Both are now summed via separate `items.reduce` calls into `policyBase`.

Current src/policy.ts:
```typescript
export interface Customer {
  yearsWithMHPCO: number;
}

export interface Item {
  type: string;
  material?: string;
  enchantment?: number;
  cursed?: boolean;
}

const PROCESSING_FEE = 5;

const BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {
  sword: 100,
  amulet: 60,
  staff: 80,
  potion: 40,
  rune: 25,
  moonstone: 25,
};

const COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;
const COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;

const CURSE_SURCHARGE = 0.5;
const HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5;
const HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE = 0.3;

const FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE = 0.1;

const roundUpToGold = (amount: number): number => Math.ceil(Math.round(amount * 100) / 100);

const premiumForType = (type: string, count: number): number =>
  count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE ? COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * BASE_PREMIUMS[type];

const curseSurchargeForItem = (item: Item): number =>
  item.cursed ? BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] * CURSE_SURCHARGE : 0;

const enchantmentSurchargeForItem = (item: Item): number =>
  (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD ? BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE : 0;

export const calculatePremium = (customer: Customer, items: Item[]): number => {
  if (items.length === 0) return PROCESSING_FEE;

  const countsByType: Record<string, number> = {};
  for (const item of items) {
    countsByType[item.type] = (countsByType[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;
  }

  const groupPremium = Object.entries(countsByType).reduce(
    (sum, [type, count]) => sum + premiumForType(type, count),
    0,
  );

  const curseSurcharge = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + curseSurchargeForItem(item), 0);
  const enchantmentSurcharge = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + enchantmentSurchargeForItem(item), 0);

  const policyBase = groupPremium + curseSurcharge + enchantmentSurcharge;
  const withSurcharge = policyBase + policyBase * FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE;
  return roundUpToGold(withSurcharge) + PROCESSING_FEE;
};
```

Refactor while keeping all tests green. Run `pnpm test:unit:basic` from /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-09-11_00-37-13_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.1-hybrid-testlist-scope-fix_sonnet-5-native-no-thinking-3 to verify.

There is now real, confirmed duplication: `curseSurcharge` and `enchantmentSurcharge` are two nearly-identical `items.reduce` calls that each sum a per-item surcharge function. This is a good candidate for Rule 3 (no duplication) — e.g., a single reduce that sums `curseSurchargeForItem(item) + enchantmentSurchargeForItem(item)` per item, or a generic "sum per-item modifier" helper. Apply whichever the tests support and is clearest; both surcharges are known to stack additively per the spec (a cursed AND highly-enchanted item gets both). Don't add loyalty/first-insurance/follow-up logic — those are separate, already-tested policy-wide modifiers, out of scope here.

